{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-60182",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since $(a,2)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=a,\\,y=2$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x-b \\\\ y=x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=a,\\,y=2$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=3a-b \\\\ 2=a+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $a$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =a+5 \\\\ -3 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $a=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3a-b \\\\ 2 & =3(-3)-b \\\\ 2 & =-9-b \\\\ 11 & =-b \\\\ -11 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-3$ and $b=-11.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
